<PageTitle>FairShare - Understanding the Alabama CS-42</PageTitle>

<HeadContent>
    <link rel="canonical" href="@FairShare.Web.PublicSite.Canonical("/guides/alabama-cs42")" />
</HeadContent>

<div class="container my-5" style="max-width: 780px;">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-1">Understanding the Alabama CS-42</h1>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
        Rule 32, Alabama Rules of Judicial Administration &middot; the rules behind
        <a href="/States/AL/CS42">FairShare's Alabama calculator</a>, computed with the
        official AOC workbooks (CS-42 Rev. 5/2022 and CS-42-S Eff. 6/2023).
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5">The big picture</h2>
    <p>
        Alabama uses the <strong>income shares</strong> model: the guideline estimates what the
        parents would spend on the children if they lived together, then splits that amount in
        proportion to each parent's income. There are two worksheets - <strong>CS-42</strong> for
        standard custody, and <strong>CS-42-S</strong> when the parents share 50/50 physical
        custody. Both work in whole dollars per month, exactly like the official workbooks.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Adjusted gross income (lines 1-2)</h2>
    <p>
        Each parent starts from <strong>monthly gross income</strong> - income from essentially any
        source - then subtracts child support actually paid under preexisting orders for other
        children (line 1a) and preexisting periodic alimony actually paid (line 1b). What remains is
        <strong>adjusted gross income</strong> (line 2), and each parent's share of the combined
        total (line 3) sets their share of everything that follows.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">The basic obligation (line 4)</h2>
    <p>
        The combined adjusted income and the number of children look up a monthly amount on
        Alabama's <strong>basic child-support obligation schedule</strong> - the table realigned in
        September 2021, which is why FairShare's results cite the vintage
        <em>"AL Realigned Sept 2021"</em>. The lookup uses the nearest $50 row; combined income
        below the schedule's first row uses the $250 row, and above the schedule's top
        ($30,000 a month) the guideline does not set an amount - that is left to the court.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Child care and health care (lines 5-10)</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Work-related child-care costs</strong> and the cost of the
        <strong>children's health-care coverage</strong> are added to the basic obligation, and the
        combined total (line 7) is split between the parents by their income shares (line 8). Each
        parent is then credited for the child-care and health-coverage costs they actually pay
        (lines 9-10), so nothing is paid twice.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">The self-support reserve (lines 11-13)</h2>
    <p>
        Since the May 2022 revision, the worksheet protects a basic amount for the paying parent's
        own needs: each parent keeps a <strong>$981-a-month self-support reserve</strong>, and only
        <strong>85%</strong> of the income above that reserve is treated as available for support -
        though never less than <strong>$50</strong>. The recommended order (line 13) is the
        <em>lesser</em> of the parent's share of the obligation and that available income, so low
        incomes produce reduced orders down to a $50 floor. A parent with no gross income at all is
        set to $0.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Who pays</h2>
    <p>
        The worksheet computes both columns, but the recommended order applies to the parent
        <em>without</em> primary physical custody. FairShare's results name that parent and
        highlight the recommended row, with every line above it showing exactly where the number
        came from.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Shared 50/50 custody (CS-42-S)</h2>
    <p>
        When the parents genuinely split physical custody 50/50, the CS-42-S applies instead. It
        raises the basic obligation to <strong>150%</strong> - two households cost more than one -
        adds both parents' child-care and health-coverage costs, and then credits each parent
        <strong>half</strong> of the shared-custody obligation for the time the children are with
        them. After those credits, the parent with the higher remaining amount pays it; when the
        two sides effectively cancel out, no money changes hands and FairShare says so rather than
        naming a payer.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">The number is a starting point</h2>
    <p>
        The guideline amount is presumed correct, but Rule 32 lets a court deviate where applying
        it would be manifestly unjust or inequitable - shared or split custody arrangements and
        extraordinary costs are among the stated reasons - and any deviation requires written
        findings. If a figure surprises you, the worksheet lines on the calculator show exactly
        where it came from.
    </p>

    <p class="text-muted small mt-4 mb-0">
        FairShare provides estimates for informational purposes only - not legal advice. Actual
        support obligations are determined by the court under your state's guidelines. See the
        <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4">
        <a href="/States/AL/CS42" class="btn btn-link">&larr; Back to the Alabama calculator</a>
    </div>
</div>
